## 4. Two options

Both fund Bing to the $30,000 already approved for it, and both remove Native. They differ in how much reduced activity is kept around that core.

| Line | Approved August | Controlled Pullback | Deep Pullback |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Bing | $30,000 | $30,000 | $30,000 |
| Azerion | $35,000 | $21,478 | $0 |
| Quantcast | $35,000 | $10,968 | $0 |
| Native | $25,000 | $0 | $0 |
| **New monthly spend** | **$125,000** | **$62,446** | **$30,000** |
| **Reduction against approved** | n/a | **50%** | **76%** |
| **Change against August delivery** | n/a | -$59,112 | -$91,558 |

Figures are monthly. Because the reduction is temporary and its length depends on the September reassessment, no multi-month total is headlined.

### Controlled Pullback, $62,446

Funds Bing to its approved weight, the one move in this document that increases spend on anything. Halves Azerion rather than removing it, because a channel producing applications at a known cost keeps a place in the mix even when that cost is high, and because the reduction can be reversed within 24 hours if the September read improves. Retires a superseded Quantcast campaign that is spending about $9,451 a month and returned no results in August, then holds the remaining productive Quantcast line at half its weight so a modelled, retargetable audience stays live rather than being rebuilt from zero. Removes Native.

The recommendation is this option. It corrects the allocation without going dark on any channel that has shown it can produce.

### Deep Pullback, $30,000

Keeps only Bing, at its approved weight. It removes all reach spend and all Azerion activity, preserving the most cash while retaining the acquisition engine that works. It accepts the cost and time of restarting upper-funnel activity from cold, and it gives up Azerion's roughly 44 applications a month.

This is the stronger choice if cash preservation outweighs volume, or if the September read shows the Quantcast line failing to improve.

### Where the two genuinely differ

The decision between them is whether about $32,000 a month is worth roughly 44 Azerion applications and a live upper-funnel audience. On current evidence those applications cost $978 each and are getting more expensive every month, while the Quantcast line records its results as view-through with click attribution not yet confirmed, and its retargeting segment is a thin and expensive share of delivery. The Controlled Pullback keeps both as monitored positions rather than proven ones. If the September read does not improve either, the Deep Pullback is the correct move.

