## 6. Market context, and why this is time-bound

The case for keeping the reduction temporary is not that September will be better. It is that current conditions are unusually quiet, and both market pricing and volatility seasonality point elsewhere for the autumn. Treating an unusually quiet August as the planning assumption for Q4 is the risk worth naming.

**Market pricing.** The VIX sits in the mid-teens, a subdued level, and the VIX futures curve is upward sloping through the end of the year. Cboe describes the futures term structure as a measure of market expectations for future volatility, so the options market is currently assigning a higher volatility premium to the autumn than to today. This is market pricing rather than a forecast.

**Seasonality.** Cboe's historical work shows volatility tending to rise from late July into October, with October historically carrying the highest average levels, before easing into year-end.

**Calendar.** Dated events fall inside the window: the FOMC meeting on September 15 to 16 with updated economic projections, the FOMC meeting on October 27 to 28, the US midterm elections on November 3, and the FOMC meeting on December 8 to 9, also with projections. There is no FOMC meeting in November.

**A caution on the VIX for this business.** The VIX measures expected S&P 500 volatility, not currency volatility. It is a reasonable proxy for the broad risk environment and nothing more. For GGMI, CME's FX CVOL indices are the more defensible measure, and MXN CVOL is more relevant here than any equity volatility index. We propose reading four layers in order: the VIX for the broad risk environment, FX CVOL for expected currency volatility, CME FX volumes for actual market participation, and FOREX.com applications and funded accounts for the business response. The first three are context. The fourth decides.

None of these indicators is an automatic budget trigger.

